commit 43594468119e80642342fc202fa751120608a524
Author: Hung Phan (Henry) <79650826+henryph24@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Oct 8 20:26:36 2026 +0700

    fix(extension): abort stale TTS fetches on a voice switch and freeze the karaoke clock while nothing plays

    The extension e2e Kokoro check failed 3 of 3 overnight runs (no highlighted
    word within 30 s of the switch), and the Supertonic check could pass while
    no Supertonic audio played.

    Causes
    - The offscreen player stopped its old session without aborting its
      fetches, so the previous voice's prefetched chunks kept the on-device
      queue busy and the new voice's first chunk waited behind them.
    - The content engine's karaoke clock extrapolated from the last time update
      while nothing played, so the highlight swept through words nobody heard.

    Changes
    - extension/offscreen.js stop() and public/js/queue.js dispose() abort every
      in-flight /api/tts fetch; api.tts takes an AbortSignal. The engine already
      drops a queued request whose client hung up.
    - extension/content/engine.js: the clock holds on any state other than
      playing until the audio reports its own time again.
    - checkVoiceSwitch in the extension e2e switches after 1.5 s of audio,
      samples the highlight every 50 ms and on each loading toggle, fails on
      any movement while loading, and counts only positions heard afterwards.
    - Unit tests for the web queue's abort signals (4).
    - CLAUDE.md records the clock invariant; tasks/todo.md records the round.

    Measurements (M3 Pro): old code under the new check moved the highlight
    through 4 positions while each voice synthesized; fixed code holds at 1.
    First Kokoro audio after the switch 1248 ms cold, 1534 ms warm.
    npm test 301 passed, 1 skipped; web e2e 14/14; extension e2e 31/31;
    desktop e2e 8/8.

    Entire-Checkpoint: 01M4DV3G4X57TWJN6967D4TPV7

